#pragma once

// ============================================================================
// DUSKLIGHT FORK FILE. This SHADOWS the RTXDI submodule's
// submodules/rtxdi/rtxdi-sdk/include/volumetrics/rtx/algorithm/volume_lighting.slangh
//
// It shadows rather than replaces: meson.build passes `-include` roots in order
// (:367 shader_directory, :371 volumetrics_include_path_string) and
// scripts-common/compile_shaders.py:41 turns each into `-I{path}` in that same
// order, so `#include "rtx/algorithm/volume_lighting.slangh"` finds this file
// first. Verified by reading both files on 2026-08-17; not exercised by a build,
// because this fork only builds on Windows.
//
// WHAT IS HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A 368-LINE COPY. Only one upstream function
// needs to change - sampleDensityField, the per-step density hook - so this file
// renames that one function out of the way with a macro, includes the submodule's
// copy verbatim by its long path (reachable through the `submodules` -I root, the
// same way `#include "nrc/include/NrcStructures.h"` is), and defines a
// replacement that calls it. Everything else in the upstream file is still the
// upstream file, so an RTXDI bump is picked up automatically instead of silently
// drifting behind a vendored snapshot, and a signature change upstream is a
// compile error here rather than a shadowed stale copy.
//
// Pinned RTXDI submodule at the time of writing: 30431e9ff173473bb5b04190723779336ad92180
// Diff this against that path's copy if anything below stops making sense.
//
// WHAT IS ADDED: rtx.dusklight.atmosphere.fogRampMode = 2 ("Exact ramp"), which
// makes the medium's extinction follow the game's own fog curve exactly instead of
// approximating it with one scalar. See documentation/DusklightAtmosphere.md §5.2.
// ============================================================================

#define sampleDensityField dusklightUpstreamSampleDensityField
#include "rtxdi/rtxdi-sdk/include/volumetrics/rtx/algorithm/volume_lighting.slangh"
#undef sampleDensityField

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DUSKLIGHT ADDITION: the game's fog ramp as an extinction field.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The console's fog is a lerp, not a medium: GXSetFog with GX_FOG_PERSP_LIN gives
// f(d) = saturate((d - start) / (end - start)) and blends the framebuffer towards
// fog_col by f. Read twice - from GXSetFog in the game's own
// libs/dolphin/src/gx/GXPixel.c, and independently from aurora's WebGPU shader at
// aurora-ao/lib/gx/shader.cpp:1562.
//
// Requiring a participating medium to reproduce T(d) = 1 - f(d) determines its
// extinction field uniquely:
//
//     T(d)     = (end - d) / (end - start)          for start <= d < end
//     sigma(d) = -T'(d) / T(d) = 1 / (end - d)
//
// so the game's fog IS a participating medium - a heterogeneous one, thin at the
// camera and diverging at the ramp's end. That is why no single scalar sigma
// expresses it, and why fogRampMode 1 needed a residual in the composite at all.
//
// This is expressed as an optical depth over a segment rather than as a point
// sample of sigma, because the segment integral is exact and closed form:
//
//     integral of 1/(end - u) du from a to b = ln((end - a) / (end - b))
//
// Consecutive raymarch steps therefore telescope to exactly ln((end - a)/(end - d)),
// where a is where the field starts (see the next paragraph), whatever the step count
// and whatever the blue-noise jitter does to the step boundaries. A point sample would
// not. Checked numerically on 2026-08-17 against four real ramps with jittered,
// nonlinearly distributed steps: the medium's opacity matched the game's f(d) to 0.000000
// wherever the ramp starts in front of the camera.
//
// WHAT THIS FIELD CANNOT DO, stated here because the obvious claim is wrong. The lower
// clamp below starts the field at max(start, 0), because a medium only exists in front
// of the camera. When start is negative - which scripted fog banks do routinely - the
// original was already -start / (end - start) fogged AT the camera, and nothing a medium
// does can reproduce fog applied before it. The medium then reproduces the ramp's shape
// from the camera onward and the composite's top-up residual adds that head start as a
// flat constant veil; the combination is still algebraically the game's own formula. The
// medium is never *denser* than the ramp in either case, which is the property that makes
// the residual safe: it can never go negative and be clamped.
//
// THE DIVERGENCE CLAMP AND WHAT IT COSTS. sigma is unbounded as d -> end, so the
// last step's optical depth would be infinite. The remaining distance is floored at
// dusklightRampMinRemaining, which the host sets to
// rtx.dusklight.atmosphere.fogRampFloor * (end - max(start, 0)) - a fraction of the
// ramp's range in front of the camera rather than of its full span, so the floor means
// the same thing whether or not the ramp starts behind the viewer. The medium's
// transmittance therefore bottoms out at exactly fogRampFloor instead of reaching zero:
// at the 0.01 default the medium closes the ramp to 99%, not 100%, and stops thickening
// over the last 1% of the visible ramp. That last 1% is closed by the composite's top-up
// residual, which is what it is for - so the clamp costs no visible fog, only the
// medium's own density (and therefore the shafts) over that last sliver.
float dusklightRampOpticalDepth(const VolumeArgs volumeArgs, float distanceA, float distanceB)
{
  const float rampStart = volumeArgs.dusklightRampStart;
  const float rampEnd = volumeArgs.dusklightRampEnd;
  // Note: 1e-4f rather than 0 so that a degenerate ramp still divides by something finite below.
  // The host floors this too, at 1e-4 of the visible range rather than at an absolute distance
  // (kMinRampFloorFraction in rtx_dusklight_atmosphere.cpp), so in practice this max never binds -
  // it is kept because a shader must not depend on a host-side clamp it cannot see. Until
  // 2026-08-18 the host instead *refused* a fogRampFloor of 0 and silently left this mode off, so
  // the two sides disagreed about whether the option's own documented minimum was legal.
  const float lastPoint = rampEnd - max(volumeArgs.dusklightRampMinRemaining, 1e-4f);

  if (!(lastPoint > rampStart))
  {
    return 0.0f;
  }

  // Note: clamped to the ramp's own range at both ends, which is what makes the field
  // exactly zero before start (the original is perfectly clear there) and exactly flat
  // past the clamp point. A negative rampStart clamps to nothing here - the raymarch's own
  // distances begin at zero - which is the head start described above.
  const float entry = clamp(min(distanceA, distanceB), rampStart, lastPoint);
  const float exit = clamp(max(distanceA, distanceB), rampStart, lastPoint);

  return log((rampEnd - entry) / (rampEnd - exit));
}

// Replaces the upstream density hook, and falls through to it in every mode but ours.
//
// The value returned is a density scalar, and the caller multiplies it by
// volumeArgs.attenuationCoefficient (for extinction) and by
// volumeArgs.scatteringCoefficient (for in-scatter), then by the step length. So to
// land a step's optical depth on the ramp's exact integral, the density has to be
//
//     tau / (sigma * stepSize)
//
// and the reference sigma divides straight back out of both products. That is why the
// in-scatter is correct as well as the transmittance, and why the host is free to keep
// attenuationCoefficient at whatever value the other consumers of the medium need -
// with one exception, which is why the host floors it (kExactRampMinSigma in
// rtx_dusklight_atmosphere.cpp): the cancellation holds only while attenuationCoefficient.x
// is above the 1e-8 reference clamp below. At exactly zero - which
// rtx.dusklight.atmosphere.densityScale = 0 used to produce - both products are zero and the
// medium disappears while every readout still reports an exact ramp.
// .x rather than a luminance because the host sets
// this medium's extinction deliberately scalar; see Derived::sigma in
// rtx_dusklight_atmosphere.h for why the fog's colour must not become a per-channel
// extinction.
//
// NOT applied to calcVolumetricAttenuation, deliberately. That function is called from
// resolve.slangh, visibility.slangh, volume_integrator.slangh and
// RtxdiApplicationBridge.slangh with no camera and with world-space origins, where a
// ramp measured radially from the camera has no meaning. Those callers keep the scalar
// medium. The consequence, stated rather than discovered: light *visibility* through
// the fog is still computed against the scalar extinction even in this mode, so a
// distant light shining through the far end of the ramp is attenuated less than the
// view ray through the same air is. UNVERIFIED how visible that is.
//
// Three more places this mode is not exact, all bounded, all covered by the composite's
// top-up residual, and all stated here so they are recognised rather than rediscovered:
//   - evalAtmosphericDensityBetween is bypassed, so the planet-shaped atmosphere's own
//     0..1 density factor is not applied. It is 1 for any segment fully inside the
//     atmosphere shell, which every step of a fog ramp measured in hundreds of units is,
//     so this only bites for a camera above the shell.
//   - calcVolumetricAttenuation clamps a step to
//     maxAttenuationDistanceForNoAtmosphere (host: transmittanceMeasurementDistance * 5)
//     when the atmosphere is off, which is indoors. A step longer than that is truncated
//     and under-attenuates. With the grid reaching the ramp's end and its steps a
//     fraction of that, the clamp is roughly 5x the half-density distance and the steps
//     are well inside it, but it is a real ceiling and not a guarantee.
//   - the noise field is bypassed. Upstream applies enableNoiseFieldDensity AFTER the
//     atmospheric factor, and this branch returns before reaching upstream at all, so
//     rtx.volumetrics.enableHeterogeneousFog does nothing while mode 2 is selected. It
//     defaults false (rtx_global_volumetrics.h), so this is inert today - but turning it
//     on in this mode would silently have no effect, which is exactly the class of thing
//     this list exists to stop someone rediscovering. Combining the two would mean
//     multiplying the ramp's density by the noise rather than replacing it.
float sampleDensityField(
  const VolumeArgs volumeArgs,
  const VolumeDefinitionCamera camera,
  float3 from, float3 to, float distance, const float timeMS
)
{
  if (volumeArgs.dusklightFogRampMode == 2)
  {
    // Note: translated world space is world space minus the volume camera's position
    // (camera.slangh, worldToTranslatedWorld), so a length here is a RADIAL distance from
    // that camera - which is the quantity THIS FORK's ramp is a function of
    // (composite.comp.slang uses hitT), so the medium and the residual agree and the
    // identity holds.
    //
    // The console's own fog is a function of perspective-corrected depth rather than
    // radial distance (aurora-ao/lib/gx/shader.cpp:1562 evaluates it on in.pos.z), and
    // GXSetFogRangeAdj exists to correct for exactly that difference at wide FOV. Neither
    // aurora backend implements the adjustment table, so radial-vs-planar is a standing
    // approximation of this whole fog path rather than something this mode introduces.
    // It over-corrects at the top and bottom of the frame relative to GX. Do NOT "fix"
    // this toward eye-space Z: the ramp, the residual and the medium must agree, and
    // radial is what the composite already uses.
    const float sigmaReference = max(volumeArgs.attenuationCoefficient.x, 1e-8f);
    const float stepLength = max(distance, 1e-6f);

    return dusklightRampOpticalDepth(volumeArgs, length(from), length(to)) / (sigmaReference * stepLength);
  }

  return dusklightUpstreamSampleDensityField(volumeArgs, camera, from, to, distance, timeMS);
}
